+# pi config — evidence log
+
+Dated evidence, spikes and numbers behind `DESIGN.md`. `DESIGN.md` holds the current decision; this file holds why, and what was tried. Append; don't rewrite history.
+
+## Decisions in full
+
+The full text of each `DESIGN.md` decision row as of 2026-11-14, with its evidence.
+
+### Agent model
+
+**Decision.** Single steerable agent + soft modes. One opt-in scout as a child `pi` process behind `bin/scout` (see Scout).
+
+**Why.** Token billing punishes context carried every turn; a child process isolates it at zero resident cost. `pi-subagents` cost 8,277 resident tokens per turn for no measured quality gain and was removed 2026-09-16.
+
+### Model config
+
+**Decision.** AUTHORITATIVE in `settings.json` (default provider/model/thinking, enabledModels) and `bin/scout` (`MODEL=` for the scout child). This document does not track model choices.
+
+**Why.** Model churn outpaced this file twice (2026-07, 2026-08); a stale table is worse than none. Core pi has no fallback chain for the main session (verified 0.84.3) — on provider quota exhaustion, switch models manually via `/model` or `--model`.
+
+### Thinking
+
+**Decision.** Default `medium`; `Shift+Tab` cycles; editor border color = live HUD.
+
+**Why.** Reliable, visible level control. Spend tokens on reasoning, save them on context.
+
+### Compaction
+
+**Decision.** Mario defaults, `keepRecentTokens` trimmed 20k→16k. Plus `extensions/compact-cap.ts`: a flat ~210k ceiling (raised from 165k 2026-09-29). At `turn_end` over the cap it only warns, once per crossing, to `/handoff` and start a fresh session; at `agent_settled` it compacts, which aborts nothing. Session command `/compact-cap [on|off|<n>k]`, 30k floor (below compaction's own output it thrashes).
+
+**Why.** Native trigger is per-model (`contextWindow - reserveTokens`), useless on 1M-window models. Until 2026-11-14 the cap compacted mid-run and prime-reminder auto-resumed the aborted run; that was retired because short sessions beat a run resumed on a summary, and compaction is where rules got lost. Cost: a run that crosses the cap keeps growing until it ends (20-50k overshoot was measured before).
+
+### Modes
+
+**Decision.** `/plan`, `/review`, `/memory`, `/challenge`, `/grill`, `/diagnose`, `/handoff` (prompt-templates, SOFT).
+
+**Why.** Reusable behavioral steers; no tool-gating. `/ask` redundant with default; `/spec` redundant with `mission`/`to-prd`. challenge/grill/diagnose are pi-native adaptations of the OpenCode challenger agent + Pocock grill-me/diagnose skills.
+
+### Questions
+
+**Decision.** `pi-ask-user` (npm) — model-invocable `ask_user` selection UI; `extensions/ask-user-args.ts` drops the empty top-level fields OpenAI models fill in next to `questions`, and turns a one-question batch padded with a blank or repeated entry back into a single question (edlsh/pi-ask-user#88, open), which 0.17.0 rejects in an endless retry loop.
+
+**Why.** One structured clarifying question beats a wrong implementation loop. `/challenge` routes questions through it explicitly (models won't pick a UI tool unprompted); `/grill` does not: it asks in chat, one wave at a time.
+
+### RTK
+
+**Decision.** `pi-rtk-optimizer` (npm), out-of-box config except rewrite notices off (`/rtk`, stored per machine in `~/.pi/agent/extensions/pi-rtk-optimizer/config.json`, so set it on the work laptop too).
+
+**Why.** Compacts noisy tool output (test/build/git/lint/grep) before it enters context. Risky read-compaction ships off. No-ops where the brew `rtk` binary is absent (`guardWhenRtkMissing`), so one shared config works on both machines. Coverage gap (measured 2026-07-20): `fetch`/`read` dumps aren't in its pattern set — a fat session was 73% tool results led by fetch+bash; that gap is context-prune's job.
+
+### Context pruning
+
+**Decision.** REMOVED 2026-07-26 (trial ended).
+
+**Why.** Live-fire found the failure mode decisive: with a quota-dead or absent summarizer it degrades to a silent no-op while appearing enabled (14/14 failed summarizer calls, zero pruned). Lifetime spend across 33 sessions was $3.38 — the risk of a silently dead cost-optimization outweighed the marginal saving. RTK plus compact-cap remain the context controls. Upstream issue unfiled by anyone as of removal.
+
+### Compaction UX
+
+**Decision.** `extensions/prime-reminder.ts`. Every compaction arms a one-shot pointer back to the rules, injected on the next turn (fail-open). Nothing resumes an interrupted run.
+
+**Why.** A pointer, not a re-injection; sessions that never compact pay nothing.
+
+### Web access
+
+**Decision.** `pi-web-access` (npm, swapped in 2026-08-11 for `pi-fetch`) — `web_search` + `fetch_content` (URL→markdown, PDF, GitHub-clone, video); `extensions/docs.ts` for live docs: one direct `docs(library, query)` tool on Context7's keyless HTTP API, replacing `@upstash/context7-pi` (2026-10-28). `web-search.json` (tracked, linked): `toolExposure: deferred` (web tools are declared only after `web_enable`), `maxInlineContentChars: 15000`, `source_check` off.
+
+**Why.** pi-fetch died by adoption (17 weekly downloads, no release since 2026-04); pi-web-access is the ecosystem standard (75k weekly, MIT, active). The old "no web search" stance is REVERSED: live experience on Claude Code showed search is a real gap, not noise. Zero-config search (keyless Exa; reuses openai-codex auth for OpenAI search at home; work falls back keyless), optional keys in `~/.pi/web-search.json`, SSRF guards built in (localhost/private IPs blocked). Smoke-verified headless on install day. 2026-10-28: context7's two tools cost 4.3k chars of schema per turn plus a 1k-char skill for 22 calls in 14 days; `docs.ts` is 400 chars and one call, and fails loud (every non-2xx, non-JSON or empty reply throws; only `no_libraries_found` is a result) so an API change is seen, not swallowed. Deferred web exposure and the 15k cap were measured: a cold `2+2` turn carried ~11.5k tokens, of which tool schemas and skill listings were the unused part; `web_search` results averaged 19k chars and stay in context for the rest of the session.
+
+### Skills
+
+**Decision.** Auto-loaded from `~/.agents/skills` (5, shared with Claude Code): `decisions`, `testing-patterns`, `git-patterns`, `pdf-images`, `map`; `d2-diagrams` excluded in `settings.json` (0 reads in 14 days, 2026-10-28). Package skills off (`pi-ask-user`: `"skills": []`). All model-invocable. `map` runs on `map.mjs` + ast-grep (2026-10-28): TS/JS/Python, tsconfig/jsconfig path aliases and barrels followed, replacing the tsc-based `digest.sh`. `map.mjs <repo> --diff <base>` lists the touched files by fan-in with edge, cycle and export changes against the working tree; `/review` runs it first.
+
+**Why.** Pruned 2026-07-05 (27 -> 10) and again 2026-09-16 (8 -> 5): deleted `context7` (superseded by the `@upstash/context7-pi` package), `security-review` (duplicates `discipline.md` gate 3), and `caveman` (its always-on clause referenced an `AGENTS.md` section that no longer existed). **Correction:** pi has no skill-listing budget — no `skillListingBudgetFraction` or `maxSkillDescriptionChars` equivalent exists in 0.85.1's schema, so "progressive disclosure" does not bound the roster. Every description is resident every turn. Keep the list short by hand.
+
+### Persona
+
+**Decision.** AGENTS files GENERATED from `agents-md/` fragments (persona + standards + ops shared; env.work overlay; ladder last) via `build-agents.sh`. Persona rewritten 2026-07-10 for Opus-4.8-era models. Restructured 2026-07-20 for salience: delegation contracts front-loaded in the persona fragments (persona-core.md shared with Claude Code + persona-pi.md pi-only; trigger catalog cut 2026-07-21 — auto-dispatch is probabilistic, contracts + explicit dispatch are not); standards compressed 163→108 lines (catalog → principles); Solution Ladder + Prime Directives digest moved to `ladder.md`, concatenated LAST in both variants.
+
+**Why.** Lost-in-the-Middle U-curve: content at prompt extremes gets used, the middle decays — the ladder sat at 65% depth, squarely in the trough. Primacy for identity + dispatch, recency for the ladder + digest. Edit fragments, never outputs — `--check` fails on drift.
+
+### Themes
+
+**Decision.** Three vendored packs in `themes/`, switch in `/settings`. Default `bearded-hc-midnightvoid`.
+
+**Why.** The five upstream theme packages were dropped; the JSON is trivial and vendoring removes five package installs from cold start. `install.sh` links the whole `themes/` directory, not one file.
+
+### Scout
+
+**Decision.** `bin/scout` wrapper spawning a minimal child `pi` (`-ne -nc -ns -np -t read,grep,find,ls,bash`, low thinking, 300 s; nesting refused via `PI_SCOUT_DEPTH`; fan-out capped in the wrapper (2026-10-28): exit 75 when two scouts are live, read from `.running/<pid>.meta`; bash 4.4+ required, refused loudly under `/bin/bash` 3.2). Read-only: the worker agent and `--rw` worker mode were both retired (2026-11-14); implementation stays in the main session.
+
+**Why.** Zero resident tool schema in the parent; ~3.5k-token child; 3–4 KB digest returned. Measured against `pi-subagents` (8,277 resident, no quality difference) and the bundled example tool (687 resident, 72–79 KB returned per call). See Scout.
+
+### MLX
+
+**Decision.** Auto-activating provider (`extensions/mlx-local.ts`, loaded). OMLX at `localhost:11434/v1`, Bearer auth via `$OMLX_API_KEY`.
+
+**Why.** Registers nothing when the server is down or the key is absent — safe to keep enabled everywhere. Env is frozen at pi launch; restart (not `/reload`) after exporting the key.
+
+### Bedrock
+
+**Decision.** Built into pi (`amazon-bedrock` provider, Converse API, auto cache points). Export `AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK` (or `AWS_PROFILE`) + `AWS_REGION`; add `amazon-bedrock/us.anthropic.claude-*` ids to `enabledModels` when adopting.
+
+**Why.** Frontier escalation without Copilot's proxy limits. Mind: 5-min cache TTL, thinking-`high` cost on opus, 1M-window sessions never auto-compact — keep Bedrock sessions short.
+
+### UI
+
+**Decision.** `pi-minimalist` (npm, pinned to 0.3.1: one-line tool rows, consecutive calls grouped, every turn's thinking and text kept in order; the `minimalist` block keeps streaming thinking open and leaves `edit`/`write` to pi's own boxes so changes are never folded away) plus `split-diff.ts`, which draws edit diffs old
+
+**Why.** new inside pi's edit box at 120+ columns using `renderSplitDiff` from `pi-tidy` (npm, pinned to 0.4.12, installed with `"extensions": []` so none of its own patches load; it reads pi's internal `preview` field and the box's child layout, guarded by `tests/split-diff.test.ts`), plus four copied files with credit headers: `polish-chrome.ts` (pi-polish 0.1.3 `chrome.ts`: ❯/● markers in column 1 with text in column 3, the gutter pi's tool rows and notices already use, which pi's own `outputPad` (0 or 1) can't give; spinner; the waiting phase keyed on `ask_user`; upstream's hidden-thinking blanking cut. 0.1.5 adds only features, no fixes), `context-bar.ts` (pi-context-bar, patched: bar fills against compact-cap's live threshold read through `lib/compact-cap-state.ts`, cwd name before the branch, heads-up cost in the `$` total, compact `▰▱ 13k/150k 9%` meter, cwd and branch flush left with cache, context, model and cost flush right; in a half split it wraps to two lines with the cost pinned top right after the model), `heads-up.ts` (pi-heads-up, patched: one note per 3+-tool task as a chat entry, thinking off, "nothing to flag" recorded but not drawn), `background.ts` (pi-background, patched; see Jobs). Clicked file links open in nvim through `config/kitty/open-actions.conf`; pi's fullscreen TUI captures the mouse and would run macOS `open` (VLC for `.ts`), so `kitty-links.ts` hands `file://` clicks to `kitty @ action open_url`. | Copies, not packages, because each needed a patch; inherited `any` stays, new lines add none. Heads-up costs ~$0.03 per note at a ~90% cache hit and its cost is shown, not hidden. Re-copy upstream fixes by hand.
+
+### Jobs
+
+**Decision.** `background.ts`: `bg_run`/`bg_status`/`bg_kill`, bash capped at 120 s default / 600 s max (scout and `timeout …` exempt), and a zero-token watchdog (`lib/watchdog.ts`, pure, tested). Foreground bash is pi's own tool with wrapped operations: no output, CPU or network for 90 s kills the process group (`killed: idle 90s …`); `ssh`/`docker`/`kubectl`/`scout`/`timeout` are exempt. `bg_run` jobs are never killed; a quiet idle job wakes pi at 5/15/45 min, a busy silent one at 30 min, at most 3 wakes. Declared signals win: `$PI_HEARTBEAT` (touch it) and `bg_run health` (checked every 30 s). Job messages are steered into a running turn (seen before its next model call) and start a turn only when pi is idle. Bash has four owners, one job each: `background.ts` caps the timeout (`tool_call`) and runs the command (re-registers pi's own bash definition with watched operations, pi's documented override pattern, so schema and renderer are unchanged); `pi-rtk-optimizer` rewrites the command text in its own `tool_call`, which runs after ours because local extensions load before packages, and the watchdog's exemptions still match the rewritten `rtk docker …` (`tests/watchdog.test.ts`); `pi-minimalist` draws the rows. Job tails (completion message, watchdog wake, `bg_status`) collapse `\r` progress redraws to the text a terminal would show, so a progress bar is one line, not thousands of chars (2026-10-28). Jobs do not outlive pi: `session_shutdown` aborts every running job's controller and clears the job table, so nothing started with `bg_run` survives the session that started it.
+
+**Why.** Every long-running path needs a limit or a health signal. Evidence first: exit, wake_on, declared signal, output, then CPU/network/process change; a failed `ps`/`nettop` never kills or wakes. `nettop` must run with `-n` (5.1 s per call without it). Spiked on 1.4.0: a 0.05 s CPU threshold killed a throttled download, so any increase counts.
+
+### Stream stalls
+
+**Decision.** `extensions/stream-watchdog.ts` (main session and scout children): no provider stream event for 5 min (10 min at xhigh/max) aborts the response and sends one "continue" message; a second stall in a row stops loudly. `bin/scout` adds an outer guard: a stale child session file with no tool running is killed after 600 s (900 s at xhigh) with exit 124.
+
+**Why.** pi's `httpIdleTimeoutMs` never reaches the Bedrock path (`bedrock-converse-stream.js` passes only the abort signal); work sessions hung 8–47 min until killed by hand. Spiked: `agent_before_settle` does not fire after an abort, so the resume goes through `agent_settled`; a fake Bedrock hang now ends in seconds instead of never. Local providers (localhost) are exempt.
+
+### Bedrock thinking
+
+**Decision.** `extensions/bedrock-reasoning.ts` adds `reasoning.summary: "auto"` to Bedrock GPT requests (main session and scouts). Use `us.`/`global.openai.gpt-5.6-luna`; the bare id fails. It also drops `reasoningContent.reasoningText` from replayed GPT assistant messages; encrypted reasoning (`redactedContent`) stays.
+
+**Why.** pi sends only `reasoning.effort`, so Converse returned no thinking text (20-char placeholder vs 373 chars on the work laptop). pi-ai replays non-Claude thinking as `reasoningText`, which Bedrock GPT rejects, so every Sol request after a tool call failed (work, 10-27). Replaces the hand-written work "mantle" extension, which defaulted to a region without the endpoint (404).
+
+### Tracking
+
+**Decision.** Files in `~/Dev/dotfiles/config/pi/`, symlinked into `~/.pi/agent/`. Pi itself is the managed install (`~/.pi/agent/install/releases/<version>`, `current-version` picks one, `~/bin/pi` → `~/.pi/agent/bin/pi` launcher; `pi update` swaps releases), NOT brew or npm-global — the brew formula lags releases (was pinned 0.80.6 when npm had 0.80.10) and nvm-global moved with the node version. `tests/pi-package.ts` follows `current-version` first, the `pi` on PATH otherwise.
+
+**Why.** Mirrors the OpenCode dotfiles pattern; version-controlled.
+
+### Placement
+
+**Decision.** Put each job in the tool that owns it (git hook, CLI, script): one job per module, few links between modules, the simplest form that works. A harness extension needs a reason: the job lives inside the harness (stream, compaction, rendering, in-loop retry) or no tool can see it. No pi extension for git or file work a hook or script can do.
+
+**Why.** Deterministic, harness-agnostic, low coupling: the same behavior under pi, Claude Code and a bare shell. The rule is in `agents-md/standards.md`, so every harness built from the fragments carries it.
+
+### Scout git guard
+
+**Decision.** `bin/guard/git` is first on PATH in every scout child. It blocks push, pull, merge, rebase, reset, clean, stash, checkout, switch, restore, commit and add, only against the scout's own repo; exit 1 is a refused command, exit 77 means the guard is misconfigured (no real git) and nothing can run.
+
+**Why.** A token scan misses wrapper spellings; PATH catches every call of `git` by name. A tripwire, not a sandbox: an absolute `/usr/bin/git` or `git -c alias.x=...` gets past.
+
+### Slice done check
+
+**Decision.** `bin/slice-check.ts` (pure logic in `lib/slice-check.ts`, tested): reads the `/plan` card on stdin, runs every done-when command, fails on changed files outside the card's files, and on test files that lost cases or assertions or gained skip/only. `--staged` runs only the test check, for a pre-commit hook.
+
+**Why.** The agent's "done" is a claim; the card's commands and the diff are evidence. Replaces worker-sensors' settle checks for main-session slices, with no harness extension: a CLI any harness or hook can run.
+
+### Worktrees
+
+**Decision.** Branches come first; worktrees only when two trees must be live (the git-patterns skill says so). worktrunk (`wt`) copies every gitignored file (reflinked) with global excludes and no include list, since `.worktreeinclude` would put a new dotfile in every repo. `config/git/bin/worktree-setup` runs from post-checkout and wt's post-start (first wins) and installs only when a lockfile differs from main's. Main is primary for env files and `worktree-setup sync` pushes them (main wins); no symlinks.
+
+**Why.** One copy path for npm and pnpm repos, near-zero disk on APFS, and agents never edit env files.
+
+## Scout
+
+**Cost visibility.** Scout sessions live under `~/.pi/agent/sessions/scout/<pid>/` because `pi-usage-widget` walks the sessions tree recursively (`data-collection.ts:456`) and nothing else: scout spend then lands in today/all-time with no widget change (verified: moving the scout files out shifted today by exactly their summed `usage.cost.total`). `/resume` reads only `sessions/<encoded-cwd>/` (`session-manager.js:550`), so the subdirectory never shows up there, and the widget's shared `timestamp:totalTokens` dedupe keeps `--fork` copies from double-counting the parent. Every usage package surveyed on 2026-09-16 (pi-usage-dashboard, @pify/usage, pi-usage-bar, pi-footer, pi-daily-cost, pi-token-usage, pi-tracker, pi-atlas) reads either in-process events or this same tree, so none needed replacing.
+
+**Visibility without tokens (`extensions/scout.ts`).** The wrapper stamps `sessions/scout/.running/<pid>.meta` (model, thinking, start, parent `PI_SESSION_FILE`, task) and writes its digest to `.running/<pid>.out`; an `EXIT` trap moves both to `.done/` with `rc` and `finished`. The extension polls once a second: `.running` drives a footer line (`⟳ scout claude-sonnet-5/low 0:42 $0.013`, cost summed from the child's own `<pid>/*.jsonl`), and `.done` files whose parent session matches become `pi.appendEntry("scout", …)` entries, which pi never sends to the model, rendered one line collapsed (model/effort · elapsed · cost · outcome · task) with the digest under ctrl+o. Ownership by session file means two pi terminals never claim each other's scouts; shell launches with no parent are discarded; a dead-pid `.running` file (SIGKILL skipped the trap) is moved to `.done` as rc 137 ("killed"), so its cost still reaches the parent. The registered-tool alternative (bundled `subagent` shape, `onUpdate` streaming, native parallel tool calls) was planned and rejected on 2026-09-16: 200 to 300 resident tokens and a dispatch re-probe for a cosmetic gain. Measured: headless parent exits normally (timer is `unref`'d) and persists the entry; interactive single and `& wait` pair each produce one entry per scout. `session_shutdown` runs one final claim because a headless parent usually exits within the poll interval of its scout finishing; before that sweep, 10 of 10 headless A/B sessions on 2026-09-16 lost their entry. The ten-minute orphan sweep now covers only SIGKILLed parents and scouts launched from a bare shell.
+
+**Scout tool budget.** `bin/scout.prompt.md` caps the child at 8 tool calls and asks for `grep -n` plus line-range reads. Before the cap, the same S1 auth-trace prompt cost one child 5 calls and 12k tokens and the next 10 calls and 97k; after it, four reps landed at 6 to 7 calls and 11k to 16k tokens with every sub-answer intact (`/Users/anshulj/Dev/pi-scout-vs-subagents-ab-2026-09-16.md`). Every correct child across 22 A/B runs finished within 7 calls, so 8 is a cap on wandering, not on coverage.
+
+Why each flag is there is recorded in `~/Dev/pi-scout-plan-2026-09-16.md` (18 gaps, each with the evidence). The ones that bit: `-e <dir>` silently loads nothing (use `index.ts`); `--no-extensions` drops the OAuth compatibility shim, which is loaded back explicitly when installed; `grep/find/ls` are opt-in builtins that only `-t` enables; Anthropic's OAuth pool rate-limits above two concurrent agents, so the delegation rule caps parallel at two.
+
+**Dispatch is the fragile half, and it is prose.** With the old rule ("dispatch before a third file read... a narrow grep: do it yourself") the parent dispatched 0/12 times on a genuine multi-file trace; every thinking trace said "dispatch is right, but I'll do a quick grep first" and momentum finished the job, pulling 25–42 KB into context. The clause was the on-ramp. The current rule in `persona-pi.md` ("decide before the first tool call; a scoping grep is the first read") went 4/4 with the scout as the first call and 6–17 KB into the parent, at +25–30 s latency. On the live config: explicit-cue trace 4/4, casual phrasing 4/4, familiar-repo multi-file trace 3/3, narrow lookup 0/3 and single-known-file edit 0/3 (no over-dispatch). A second erosion was then measured and closed: after the digest the parent re-read cited files whole (2/4 runs, 13–22 KB); "re-read a cited line range only when you need the exact text, never the whole file" took that to 0/4 and 3–16 KB. Explicit "use the scout" dispatch was 4/4 under both rules. Re-run this probe (`/tmp/spike/run6.sh` shape: headless parent, `-nc --append-system-prompt <AGENTS variant>`, unfamiliar-package trace prompt) whenever the Delegation fragment changes.
+
+**Worker mode, retired 2026-11-14.** `--rw` (2026-09-30) ran a scout as an editing worker under `extensions/worker-sensors.ts` (files-allowed, bash blocks, settle checks, `verdict.json`). It was retired with auto runs: agentic loops and subagent implementation did not pay off in practice, and implementation now happens in short main-session slices. What survived: `bin/guard/git` (now read-only rules) and the settle checks' idea, as `bin/slice-check.ts`. The history is in git before this date.
+
+The bundled `examples/extensions/subagent` was measured as the alternative (687 resident tokens, typed single/parallel/chain) and rejected: it returns the child's whole streamed transcript as the tool result, 72–79 KB per dispatch against the wrapper's 3–4 KB, and its child inherits the full ~25k prompt.
+
+Injection-surface isolation (scout on a non-Anthropic model so untrusted content cannot steer the parent's model family) remains abandoned: every non-Anthropic slot was verified dead on 2026-09-16 (`opencode-go` 429 monthly limit, `openai-codex` account-unsupported or usage-limited, `github-copilot` `model_not_supported`). `--model` on the wrapper is the repoint when one comes back.
+
+## Opus 4.8 migration (2026-07-10)
+
+The pre-migration Opus-4.6 config is preserved at git tag `pi-config-opus-4.6` (`git show pi-config-opus-4.6:config/pi/AGENTS.work.md`). The blind-test protocol comparing 4.6/4.8 on old/new prompts was deferred — the 4.8 config was adopted directly; if the register regresses, add ONE line naming the specific behavior to `agents-md/persona.md`, don't restore sections.
+
+## Measurement
+
+Behavioural changes are measured, not asserted. The harness lives in `agent-eval/` (probes + `parse_probes.py`); the 2026-09-15/16 audit ran 128 arm-isolated runs plus a 24-run before/after on the real config. Two cautions learned there: arm isolation needs both `HOME` and `PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR` (skills come from the former), and `~/.agents/skills` is a symlink into this repo, so `cp -R` copies the link and lets a patched arm write through into the real config. Use `cp -RL`.
+
+### What measurement actually showed (2026-09-16)
+
+- **Prompt size is not a cost lever.** Deleting `AGENTS.md` cost 3 review-rubric categories, failed the headless close rule (no question at the end of a `-p` run) 83% of the time, and raised output tokens 29%.
+- **Wording changes are mostly noise.** Review recall did not move between the full prompt and four trimmed variants (9.11 vs 9.56 of 12, n=9 per arm, p=0.378). Rubric categories 8 (falsy cache-hit) and 11 (`node-fetch` vs native) were missed by every arm in all 25 review runs — model blind spots, not prompt defects. Do not rewrite fragments hoping to fix those.
+- **Structure prompts work; the effect is compliance, not judgment.** Promoting the `decisions` skill moved Goal-Options-Plan structure from 2.83 to 6.50 of 7 (p=0.0022, perfect separation). But the *pick never changed* in any run, and the scorer rewarded a template this repo prescribed. Treat it as "the reasoning is now visible", not "the reasoning is now better".
+- **A rule stated twice in prose still failed 30% of the time.** Enforcement in code took it to 0 of 8. Prefer a check to another sentence.
+- **Cold start, 2026-10-28.** A fresh session's first turn carried ~11.5k tokens: AGENTS.md 12k chars, tool schemas ~16.5k chars (`ask_user` 4.2k, context7 4.3k, web tools 3.5k), skill listings 4.2k chars. Removing only what had no use in 14 days (context7's schema+skill, web tools until `web_enable`, `source_check`, `d2-diagrams`, the ask-user skill) measured on opus-5-5 with the same one-word prompt, before/after: 12,548 -> 10,545 tokens (-16%); system prompt 21,995 -> 20,198 chars; declared tools 19 -> 14. A haiku preview had projected ~8.2k; token counts differ by model, so compare arms on one model only. The base is ~11% of an average opus turn (~105k cached), so the 16% cut is ~2% of spend; session length is the cost lever. AGENTS.md was left alone (see Rejected).
+- **Turns, 2026-10-28.** Over 14 days, 4,096 of 4,419 assistant turns with tool calls carried exactly one call (93%, avg 1.1; 3,353 single `bash`), although pi's system prompt and `ops.md` both ask for batching. Parked: no harness code can merge the model's calls, and the share of those single calls that did not depend on the previous result is unmeasured. Classify a sample before adding any rule.
+- **Effects are model-specific.** The headless close violation (a question at the end of a `-p` run) never occurred on `claude-fable-5-1` or `opus-5` — only on `sonnet-5`. The enforcing extension was retired 2026-10-27; the rule now lives in the prompt fragments.
